---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/url';
---

<Layout
  title="Start Here"
  description="How to use this handbook, and three reading paths matched to your background."
>
  <h1>How to use this handbook</h1>
  <p>
    Everything here assumes no prior background — not in marketing, not in AI. Terms
    are defined the first time they matter, not before. If a page uses a concept from
    an earlier page, it says so at the top, with a link.
  </p>

  <h2>Three reading paths</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Front to back.</strong> The handbook is written as a sequence — each part
    builds on the last. Start at <a href={href('foundations/the-problem')}>The Problem</a>
    and follow the "Where this leads" link at the end of every page.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>By workflow.</strong> If you already know roughly what you're looking for —
    how discovery works in an AI-mediated search era, say, or how journeys get
    orchestrated — jump straight to that workflow's page. Prerequisite chips at the top
    tell you what to read first if you're missing context.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>By case.</strong> If you learn better from a worked example than from a
    framework, start with a case study and follow its cross-links back into the
    workflow pages it draws on.
  </p>

  <h2>What "done reading" looks like</h2>
  <p>
    There's no quiz at the end. You'll know the material landed when you can look at a
    real marketing failure — your own organization's or someone else's — and name which
    of the seven workflows broke, and why. That's the actual skill this handbook is
    teaching.
  </p>
</Layout>
